WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.667
Tonight, Americans already
slammed by soaring prices

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:06.060
now getting hit even harder.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.240
New numbers showing inflation
surging to its fastest pace

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.670
in nearly 40 years.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.860
The consumer price index, or CPI,

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.420
tracks the cost of everyday items.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.670
It's jacked up a whopping
6.8% in the last year,

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.800
the biggest increase in almost 40 years.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.800
Gas is up 51%, beef is up
20%, and furniture by 11%.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.650
I think probably in June,

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.640
it was about, a dollar is worth a dollar.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.130
So now that dollar is
worth about seventy cents.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.510
This has made the "Nightly News"

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:41.030
sound a little too much like
a rerun of "That '70s Show,"

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:44.940
a sitcom set in a decade
famous for ever rising prices

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.560
and useless Whip Inflation Now buttons

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.860
that Gerald Ford tried
to stick on everybody

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.590
from running mate, Bob Dole,

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.260
to former Beatle George Harrison.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.110
Back in the 1970s, President
Ford was better known

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.710
for literally and figuratively stumbling

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.610
than for his economic acumen.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.920
Especially when it came to inflation,

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.810
which soared into double digits

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.490
shortly after he took office.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.630
Ford believed that the
way to beat inflation

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.650
was to brow beat companies
into keeping prices down

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.640
and Americans into
becoming bargain hunters.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.730
He declared a war on high prices,

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:22.857
referring to inflation as
domestic enemy number one.

00:01:22.857 --> 00:01:25.547
"I pledge to my fellow
citizens that I will buy,

00:01:25.547 --> 00:01:28.547
"when possible, only those
products and services

00:01:28.547 --> 00:01:32.510
"priced at or below present
levels," promised Ford.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.700
Joe Biden is looking reminiscent
of the 38th president,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.280
and not simply because he also has trouble

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.780
with airplane steps.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.660
Biden, his advisors and
champions in the press

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.960
are ignoring the tough lessons of the past

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.820
by downplaying inflation

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.030
or bizarrely claiming it
only freaks out rich people.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.360
Then there's leading Democrats,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.990
like Senator Elizabeth Warren,

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.120
who seem to be channeling
Gerald Ford when she tweeted out

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.300
that the reason your
Thanksgiving groceries

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.240
cost more this year was
because greedy corporations

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.980
are charging Americans extra

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.370
just to keep their stock prices high.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:06.720
Even worse, Biden and crew

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.690
are delusionally pronouncing
that we can tame inflation

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:13.330
by pumping massive amounts
of money into the economy.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.540
A course of action that
will almost certainly

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.490
make everything more expensive.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:18.970
What Ford, Biden, Warren,

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.080
and Treasury Secretary
Janet Yellen have in common

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.600
is a failure to understand

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.526
inflation's most important
underlying cause,

00:02:26.526 --> 00:02:29.950
which the Nobel prize winning
economist, Milton Friedman,

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:33.950
was explaining with unique
clarity back in the 1970s.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.670
To our understanding
the cause of inflation,

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.470
is to recognize that it
is always and everywhere,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.620
a monetary phenomenon.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.310
We've seen massive increases
in government spending

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:47.950
over the past two years,

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.120
which have been paid for by printing money

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.350
and historic increases
in the money supply.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.540
And when you print money,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.110
it means that there are more dollars

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.770
chasing the same amounts
of goods and services,

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.930
which causes prices to rise.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.180
In just the past two fiscal years,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.120
federal spending has swollen
to nearly $7 trillion a year,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.623
up from about $4.5 trillion in 2019.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.500
That money has to be paid for eventually

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.170
by the American people one way or another.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.100
Traditionally, that means higher taxes,

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.470
cuts in services, and inflation,

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.640
or a combination of the three.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.070
The real tax on the American people

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.930
is what government spends.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.660
And politicians generally
prefer printing money

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:32.090
to raising taxes or cutting services.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:33.793
Inflation is a tax,

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.020
which is imposed without representation

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.600
and which nobody has to vote for.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.140
And of course, it's a marvelous
tax from the point of view

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.520
of a Congressman trying to meet

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.210
the demands of his
constituents for more spending.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:51.020
Politicians and government economists

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.970
will even claim that the
way to drive down inflation

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.080
is by handing out more goodies.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.760
Yeah.
What this package will do

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.870
is lower some of the most important costs,

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.150
what they pay for health
care, for childcare,

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.183
and it's anti inflationary
in that sense as well.

00:04:08.183 --> 00:04:10.120
- When the President and his advisors

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.560
touted the passage of his $1.2
trillion infrastructure bill,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.685
and promised that even more was on the way

00:04:16.685 --> 00:04:20.040
through the Build Back Better
bill and other legislation,

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.180
they insisted that spending more money

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.070
will somehow make
everything more affordable

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.320
by increasing long-term economic capacity.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.760
- According to economic experts,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.260
this bill is gonna ease
inflationary pressures,

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:34.820
lowering the cost of working families.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:39.720
17, excuse me, yes, 17
Nobel laureates in economics

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.370
wrote a letter to me saying,

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.920
this is gonna affect, bring
inflation down, not up.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:46.740
- That's precisely backward,

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.420
because the government will
be printing even more money

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.460
to pay for Biden's bill.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.130
As the government borrows more money

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.410
to pay for its spending,

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.940
and the Fed prints money to buy that debt,

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:59.840
the Central Bank's balance
sheet has more than doubled

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.131
between March 2020 to November 2021.

00:05:03.131 --> 00:05:05.970
The result is that the supply
of dollars has increased

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:09.110
by nearly 40% over the past two years,

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.210
which is an off the charts record.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.440
But there's no reason to believe

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.400
that Joe Biden or anyone
else in Washington

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.610
is committed to the type of
fiscal and monetary discipline

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.640
that would actually tame inflation.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.030
In fact, his infrastructure bill

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.780
jacks the very entitlement spending

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.630
that is the major driver
of long-term spending.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.960
Not only is he trying to expand access to

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.460
and coverage offered by existing programs,

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.250
such as Medicare and Medicaid,

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.760
he's pushing for tax credits for parents,

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.100
and new entitlements for
childcare and universal preschool.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.560
Another thing that's changed
over the past 40 years

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.100
is that the publicly held debt

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.450
has more than quintupled
as a share of GDP.

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.220
In 2022, the Congressional Budget Office

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.340
predicts that interest
payments on the debt

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.640
will be 5.7% of total spending.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:00.591
And then it will more than
double to 11.6% by 2031.

00:06:00.591 --> 00:06:03.740
And that's assuming
interest rates stay low.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.870
Squeezing inflation out of the economy

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:07.350
by hiking interest rates

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:10.330
is never a popular political stance.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.150
Since it will absolutely devastate

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.770
the government's balance sheet,

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:15.810
it'll be even harder this time.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.450
- Inflation is just like alcoholism.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:21.290
In both cases, when you start drinking,

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.320
or when you start printing too much money,

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.480
the good effects come first,

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:27.630
and the bad effects only come later.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.610
- And get this. Even the
price of booze is going up.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.040
So not only will we have more sorrows,

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:35.183
it's gonna cost us more to drown them.

